Yes — in that revision this is a bug in `@apply`.

`border-dotted` is emitted twice:

```css
.border-dotted { border-width: 0; } /* reset */
.border-dotted { border-style: dotted; }
```

For normal utility classes, the generated CSS ordering also places `.border` between those rules, so `class="border border-dotted"` ends up with a 1px dotted border.

But `@apply` expands every matching `.border-dotted` rule into the target, producing:

```css
.probe {
  border-width: 0;
  border-style: dotted;
}
```

So the width reset survives and removes the visible border. It’s caused by duplicate utility selectors and `findMixin` collecting both.

A practical workaround in that version is:

```css
.probe {
  @apply border;
  border-style: dotted;
}
```

The proper fix is for `@apply` to avoid that reset rule (or, more fundamentally, avoid generating duplicate selectors for a single utility).
